## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

Resource memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` (and `reference`) plus type-specific extensions:

- **`url`** (required) — canonical URL (`^https?://`)
- **`accessed`** — ISO date when first cited (used by link-rot warnings)
- **`content-type`** — MIME type OR human-meaningful kind (paper / talk / spec / dataset)
- **`archive-url`** — archived snapshot URL for link-rot defense
- **`abstract`** — 1–3 sentence content summary
- **`license`** — license string (CC-BY-4.0 / MIT / proprietary)
- **`version`** — version string where versioning matters

{{slot-table}}

## Audit invariants

- **`resource-has-url`** (error) — every resource must declare `url:` matching `^https?://`
- **`resource-stub-promotion-candidate`** (warning, future) — stubs with ≥ N citers AND non-trivial body content are promotion candidates
- **`resource-accessed-field-stale`** (info, future) — stubs whose `accessed:` is > 1 year old

## Canonical body structure

Most resources have **no body**.  When body content exists:

```markdown
## What this resource is

<One paragraph naming the resource and its content (when
frontmatter `description:` isn't enough).>

## Why we cite it

<What claim or design choice this resource grounds.>

## Notes

<Research notes, quotes, or links to related corpus memorials.>
```

## Indexing at scale

The `resources/` subtree may be excluded from primary corpus indices by default — the primary index stays focused on substantive memorials.  BM25F retrieval may de-prioritize `resources/` entries; authors searching for content don't get drowned by resource pointers.

## Pitfalls

- **Re-implementing the source** — a resource that duplicates external content rots; keep thin, link out
- **Cargo-culting metadata** — only declare metadata that actually matters for the citation
- **Over-reification** — casual one-off body links shouldn't reify into resources
- **Stale `accessed:` dates** — bump when re-validating

